        <div id="make-boba" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Make a Boba Protein Shake With Pearls?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shake one scoop with 8 to 12 oz of cold milk or a milk and water mix, pour it over ice and add cooked pearls. Lunar Lifts' Taro Ube bag suggests 8 to 10 oz of half water, half milk, and its vegan Brown Sugar page suggests 16 oz of the same mix for the best blend. Milk makes any of these creamier; water keeps calories down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tapioca pearls vs konjac pearls</h3>

          <p>
            Traditional tapioca pearls are cassava starch, so they add carbohydrate and calories to an otherwise low-sugar drink, especially if they are soaked in brown sugar syrup. That is fine after a hard workout but undoes much of the point if you chose the powder for its low sugar. Konjac pearls are the low-calorie swap: Boba Nutrition and Lunar Lifts both sell konjac-based pearls, and Lunar Lifts says its instant brown sugar pearls have up to 70% fewer calories than traditional boba. Check the pearl label, not just the powder label, when you count.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot or iced?</h3>

          <p>
            Whey isolate is happier cold. Very hot liquid can make whey clump and foam, so if you want a hot milk tea, warm the milk gently, mix a little of it into the powder to make a paste first, then add the rest. Tuk Tuk Go recommends a shaker or blender and letting the drink sit for about a minute so the foam settles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The DIY version</h3>

          <p>
            You can also make a milk tea protein drink with any unflavored or vanilla whey and strongly brewed black tea, cooled, plus milk. It costs less per gram and lets you control caffeine and sweetness, though it rarely tastes as close to a shop drink as the powders built for it. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">vanilla protein powder</a> guides list good bases, and a <a href="/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/">grass-fed whey</a> keeps the ingredient list as short as Boba Tea Protein's.
          </p>
        </div>

